n.vm.debruijn/project-datoms is the lift\"]]]])\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1063:(defn project-datoms-counted\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1064:  \"`project-datoms` plus :mints, the number of record mints the walk\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1068:   exactly `project-datoms`'s.\"\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1083:(defn project-datoms\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1104:  (dissoc (project-datoms-counted datoms) :mints))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc:1566:                                                  (project-datoms (conj frame datum)))}\n    20\t\n    21\t## 1. Architectural position and invariants\n    22\t\n    23\tThe projection is an interpreter over plain AST datoms. It does not execute\n    24\tvalues, primitives, streams, continuations, or effects. It does not change the\n    25\tAST schema, AST walker, semantic VM, linearizer, `dao.stream`, `dao.lease`, or\n    26\tthe waitset.\n    27\t\n    28\tAll state is explicit in the forward-step state: input cursor, current graph\n    29\tframe, indexed facts, scope stack, occurrence memo, pending output, and output\n    30\tcursor. There is no namespace atom, callback, timer, registry, or clock. A\n    31\tblocked input is returned as an outcome; it is never hidden execution.\n    32\t\n    33\tThe input domain is a fully macro-expanded Universal AST. An unexpanded macro\n    34\tcall site is a terminal diagnostic: an application whose operator is a\n    35\t`:lambda` carrying `:macro? true` is unexpanded. A free variable whose name\n   202\t\n   203\t## 6. Stream composition and consumers\n   204\t\n   205\tImplementation belongs in `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`: it consumes the\n   206\texisting `:yin/*` schema and shares no transport code. It exposes:\n   207\t\n   208\t* `project-datoms`: pure projection of one complete rooted graph to Merkle\n   209\t  records and root fingerprint;\n   210\t* `forward-step`: ordinary `dao.stream` forward interpretation. It frames at\n   211\t  the root marker, projects there, emits the projected tuples, and reports a\n   212\t  partial frame at end as diagnostic. Pending writes remain explicit and are\n   213\t  retried only by host cadence.\n   214\t\n   215\tNamed storage, renderers, Datalog queries, source maps, and diagnostics consume\n   216\tthe named stream. Deduplication, cache lookup, and alpha-equivalence consume\n   217\tthe projected segment/fingerprint. The semantic VM and `yin.vm.linearize`\n   218\tcontinue to consume the named AST. A waitset is optional only for a composition\n   219\tthat owns several independent waiters; no lease fact, lease timer, callback,\n   220\tor scheduler is introduced here.\n   255\t### D4 — stream adapter\n   256\t\n   257\tImplement blocked, transport-error, invalid input, pending output, root\n   258\tframing, adjacent graphs (including graphs that reuse temporary eids), and\n   259\tterminal diagnostic outcomes. End-of-stream with a partial graph must be\n   260\tcovered on every host.\n   261\t\n   262\t### D5 — pipeline and storage integration\n   263\t\n   264\tWire Yang's post-emission path before projected persistence. Store named and\n   265\tprojected segments separately; use only the Merkle fingerprint for dedupe.\n   266\tDo not alter named consumers or runtime layers.\n   267\t\n   268\t### D6 — end condition\n   269\t\n   270\tAll D0–D5 criteria pass; alpha-equivalent programs have identical semantic\n   271\tprojected tuples and root hashes; non-equivalent fixtures remain distinct;\n   272\tall emitter types and stream outcomes are covered; and AST, walker, VM,\n   273\tlinearizer, named storage, lease, and waitset diffs are unchanged.\n   274\t\n   275\t## 8. Test matrix\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
